{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall keep all the commandment.</b> This is the commandment of setting up the stones that he goes on to mention: 'and you shall set up for yourself great stones,' that is, from within the Jordan. And these stones were for a sign and a remembrance of the miracle of the Jordan. And He commanded to write upon them the entire Torah, and they were twelve, as it is written in the book of Joshua (Joshua 4:4–5), one man from each tribe, and it is written (ibid.): 'And Joshua said to them: Pass over before the ark of YHWH your Elohim into the midst of the Jordan, and take up every man of you a stone upon his shoulder, according to the number of the tribes of the children of Israel.' And he did this for a sign and a remembrance that the waters of the Jordan were cut off before the ark when it passed through the Jordan. And these twelve stones that they took from within the Jordan they carried with them to the lodging-place, which is the Gilgal, where Joshua set them up. And it is written (ibid.): 'For your children shall ask tomorrow, saying: What are these stones to you? Then you shall tell them that the waters of the Jordan were cut off before the ark of the covenant of YHWH.' And it was necessary to say 'great,' so that they could contain the entire Torah. And besides these twelve stones that they brought out from within the Jordan and that they transported to the Gilgal, we find twelve other stones that they set up within the Jordan, as it is said (ibid. 4): 'And Joshua set up twelve stones in the midst of the Jordan, under the standing-place of the feet of the priests,' etc., 'and they are there unto this day.' And furthermore we find other stones with which they built an altar on Mount Ebal — and this is what Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote in his commentary on the Humash: 'you thus find that there were three kinds of stones,' end quote. And he said likewise on the basis of what they said in tractate Sotah: 'There were three kinds of stones — one that Moses set up across the Jordan, as it is said (Deuteronomy 1:5): \"Across the Jordan, in the land of Moab, Moses undertook,\" etc. — this is derived by a gezerah shavah of באר / באר: it is written here \"באר היטב\" (well expounded), and there (ibid.) \"Moses undertook (הואיל) באר\" — just as there, in seventy languages, so too here, in seventy languages; and one that Joshua set up within the Jordan; and one that he brought out from within the Jordan and they transported to the Gilgal.' Now according to the plain sense of the verses it appears that the stones were all twenty-four in number: twelve stones that they brought out from within the Jordan, and twelve that Joshua set up in the Jordan — those that Joshua set up in the Jordan remained there unto this day, and the reason he set them up there was so that there would be a place for the priests, the bearers of the ark, to stand upon them and their feet would not sink in the mud. And those that they brought out from within the Jordan — with them he first built an altar on Mount Ebal, and they wrote the entire Torah upon them, and afterwards they transported them to the Gilgal. If so, the sense of the verse here is: 'and you shall set up for yourself great stones' — they are the twelve that they brought out from within the Jordan — and behold this is a general statement followed afterwards by a specification. And it says: 'And it shall be, when you cross the Jordan, you shall set up these stones' — the twelve that I mentioned — 'you shall set them up on Mount Ebal,' and you shall plaster them after you build an altar there, and then 'you shall write upon the aforementioned stones all the words of this Torah באר היטב.' For if we do not explain it thus, that the passage is a general statement and a specification, I see a mixing of passages here. For how can it say 'and you shall write upon the stones all the words of this Torah באר היטב,' when it already said first 'and you shall write upon them all the words of this Torah when you cross'? And furthermore, how can it say first 'and you shall write upon them' and afterwards 'and you shall build there an altar' — when surely they built the altar first and afterwards wrote the Torah upon it in its entirety? Therefore I have concluded that the passage is a general statement and a specification, and so it is explicitly stated there in tractate Sotah, that after they built the altar from them, they dismantled the altar and took the stones and came and spent the night in their place."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:35.001225634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}